<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stoicism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stoicism is a school of Hellenistic philosophy founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-citium">Zeno of Citium</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> around 300 BCE, named after the Painted Stoa where he taught. It held that virtue, living in agreement with nature and reason, is the only true good and that passions arising from false judgements should be overcome. Taken up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> such as Seneca, Epictetus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, it greatly influenced Christian and modern thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stoicism.jpg" alt="Stoicism" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo Monti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paolo_Monti_-_Servizio_fotografico_(Napoli,_1969)_-_BEIC_6353768.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
